WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.970
We are subverting illegitimate authority

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:09.420
WikiLeaks released 76,000 classified field reports of
U.S. operations in Afghanistan.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.084
He has done a lot of harm to the United States of America and he should pay for that.

00:00:13.084 --> 00:00:16.126
I hope he rots in hell. Starting with that.

00:00:16.126 --> 00:00:18.900
WikiLeaks walks like a hostile intelligence service

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.350
and talks like a hostile intelligence service.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.480
Publish true information that is otherwise unsayable.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.590
Joe Biden to Julian Assange is a hi-tech terrorist.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:34.630
You cannot trust a government to implement the policies that it says that it's implementing.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:38.020
He has to answer for what he has done.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.149
And so we must provide the underlying tools.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.780
Many establishment journalists in the U.S. consider Julian Assange to be a criminal

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.610
whose work doesn't fit in the same category as their own.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:55.280
No journalism here. What we have is an act of espionage.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:01.000
The wholesale dumping of WikiLeaks actually isn't journalism.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.300
If you help in these stealing of classified material

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.580
nothing about the First Amendment is gonna insulate you from charges that you stole,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.490
regardless of whether or not you publish it.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:12.450
I mean, you learn that day one in news business school.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.080
In April 2019, police dragged the WikiLeaks founder out of the Ecuadorian embassy

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.754
where he lived for seven years after the U.S. government indicted him

00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:24.780
for allegedly helping Chelsea Manning access government databases.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.360
The New York Times editorial board applauded the move,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.080
writing that it, "could help draw a sharp line "between legitimate journalism and dangerous cybercrime,"

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.960
and said that the Trump administration has begun well

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.173
by charging Mr. Assange with an indisputable crime.

00:01:39.173 --> 00:01:44.357
"Julian Assange is not a freepress hero," The Washington post editorial board opined,

00:01:44.357 --> 00:01:47.740
"and he is long overdue for personal accountability."

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.910
Many in the media revile Assange and WikiLeaks

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.800
for publishing the DNC and Clinton campaign chairman,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.250
John Podesta's, leaked emails before the 2016 election.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:03.310
He hacked into the Democrats' computers and that's why Trump won, basically.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.460
Timing it right before the debate.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.920
We have to acknowledge how political WikiLeaks and Julian are being here.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.150
The disdain is mutual, with Assange regularly describing the corporate press

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.530
as complicit in hiding vital information from the public.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:21.530
History is shaped and distorted by the media.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:26.530
It is our single greatest impediment to advancement.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:31.894
Then in May 2019, the U.S. government unsealed a second set of charges against Assange,

00:02:31.894 --> 00:02:35.920
that if they were to result in a conviction, could set a dangerous legal precedent

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.040
that would put all investigative journalists who expose state secrets at risk of going to prison.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.115
Whether or not the media considers Assange one of their own,

00:02:45.115 --> 00:02:51.240
his fate could have a profound impact on the future of their profession.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.340
The government is now charging Assange with violating the Espionage Act of 1917

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.190
by publishing the information leaked by Chelsea Manning.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.900
If convicted, he could face up to 175 years in prison.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.130
Edward Snowden, a former government contractor,

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:09.400
has also been charged under the Espionage Act for leaking information to the media,

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.490
which is how it's more commonly used.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.810
What's different about Assange's case is that the government is claiming

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:19.480
an individual unaffiliated with the government is guilty of a criminal violation

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.760
for seeking out and publishing classified information,

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.140
which is exactly what journalists do on a routine basis.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:30.860
The Trump administration today just put every journalistic institution in this country

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.840
on Julian Assange's side of the ledger, which I know is unimaginable.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.168
Even many of his biggest media critics are concerned by the additional charges.

00:03:40.168 --> 00:03:43.500
Really anybody who is concerned about press freedom

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:50.389
should be deeply concerned about the prosecution of Julian Assange.

00:03:50.389 --> 00:03:55.410
Freedom of the Press Foundation cofounder, Trevor Timm, who testified in Assange's UK extradition hearing,

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:59.342
says that Assange's conviction under the Espionage Act would set a precedent

00:03:59.342 --> 00:04:04.540
that could endanger any journalist publishing leak information about the U.S. government.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.120
Maybe journalists don't like Julian Assange

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.900
or they have criticized many of his actions over the years.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:11.950
That's all well and good.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:15.480
But what really matters is the acts for which

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.550
the Justice Department's trying to criminalize here.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:22.100
Bob Woodward, for the past 40 years, has made his entire career off writing books

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.160
on various administrations' national security policies.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:29.020
He had a book called, "Obama's Wars," for example, that came out in 2010.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.980
It is hundreds of pages of highly classified information.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:36.190
Basically the most sensitive information that you could possibly imagine

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:40.270
at a far higher classification level than anything WikiLeaks published.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:44.490
The investigation that made Bob Woodward the most famous journalist in the United States

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:49.910
that led to the resignation of Richard Nixon, Bob Woodward and his partner, Carl Bernstein,

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:54.610
engaged in what the government is calling a conspiracy in the Assange case.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:05:01.160
They were going to grand jurors' houses, for example, and trying to convince them to hand over information

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.050
that it would be illegal for the grand juror to hand over.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:09.730
If this precedent was around during the Richard Nixon era it's possible that Woodward and Bernstein

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:15.050
would have been stopped far before Watergate and deep throat became household names.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.000
Richard Nixon may never have had to resign.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.780
They quite possibly could have gone to jail.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.840
I shall resign the presidency, effective at noon tomorrow.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.610
The government claims that WikiLeaks crossed a legal line

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:31.060
by posting a list of most wanted classified documents

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:37.320
and providing the encrypted Dropbox that Manning would use to submit the Iraq and Afghanistan war logs.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.940
But Timm says this too is standard journalistic practice.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.290
Freedom of the Press Foundation, the organization I run,

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.830
we have a software project called SecureDrop.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:50.530
And SecureDrop is essentially an open source version of a whistleblower submission system.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.480
Something that WikiLeaks had a hand in pioneering

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:55.900
back in the early 2000s and 2010s.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.690
Journalists at major newspapers around the country

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:02.930
and around the world are constantly asking sources to leak them information.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:03.860
Of course they are.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:07.820
And they're increasingly doing this in the internet age in a very public way.

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.190
But when the New York Times launched their SecureDrop,

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.570
they took out a full page ad in their own paper.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:17.810
I wrote a piece in The Guardian saying that somebody should leak the torture report back in 2014,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.000
if the government wasn't gonna release it in full

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.610
because it contained evidence of illegal and unconstitutional acts.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:29.430
So this is a common tactic by reporters, by newspapers, by common citizens everywhere.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:32.750
And thankfully, at least for now, it's covered under the First Amendment.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:38.054
And I'm worried if this case goes forward, that it wouldn't be.

00:06:38.054 --> 00:06:40.500
One of the key accusations in the case against Assange

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:45.420
is that he violated the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act by allegedly offering to help Chelsea Manning

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:49.980
crack a password to a government database in an effort to cover her tracks.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:53.790
On this count, many journalists have sided squarely with the government.

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:58.230
The government seems to be hanging their hat on this single, quite weak charge,

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:02.580
which relates to the 30 second conversation between Julian Assange and Chelsea Manning.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:03.840
But nothing ever came of it.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.980
I think this one charge is potentially a side show trying to convince the judge

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:13.213
that Assange is some sort of hacker and that this doesn't relate to journalism.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:17.870
While recognizing that Assange's case is vital to the cause of press freedoms,

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:22.810
many journalists have treated him with disdain, often portraying his years long confinement

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.560
in the Ecuadorian embassy as self imposed.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:30.780
A ploy to dodge sex crime charges in Sweden, which were dropped in November 2019.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:36.007
Assange defended himself by claiming he was seeking
asylum from extradition to the U.S.,

00:07:36.007 --> 00:07:41.210
where he would be indicted on charges that would seek to deny him First Amendment protections.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.160
A prediction that's been borne out.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:47.560
The years of confinement have taken a toll on his mental and physical health.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.720
In 2018, doctors determined that Assange's condition

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:53.750
was deteriorating after years of confinement,

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:57.020
and asked that he be allowed to safe passage to a hospital.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:58.890
That request was denied.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:03.114
In 2019, the UN Special Rapporteur on Torture, Nils Melzner,

00:08:03.114 --> 00:08:07.790
 described the conditions Assange has been subjected to as psychological torture.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:12.920
A decade ago, Assange was well regarded in establishment circles.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:18.160
The standing ovation he received at this 2010 Ted Talk is inconceivable today.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:24.710
Assange created WikiLeaks in 2006 and leaked documents about the inner workings of Guantanamo Bay,

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:29.320
vice presidential candidate Sarah Palin's emails, and bank fraud in Iceland.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:33.204
The organization first grabbed widespread public attention with this.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:46.150
Video WikiLeaks with title, "Collateral Murder," showed footage from a U.S. Army Apache helicopter

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:51.820
of soldiers gunning down more than a dozen people in Baghdad who weren't engaged in active combat,

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.830
including two Reuters reporters.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:57.550
The video generated international press and controversy.

00:08:57.550 --> 00:09:02.390
Assange said his intention was to expose the casual carnage and destruction happening

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:04.670
in the course of the U.S. war in Iraq.

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:08.607
It was the, "Another day at the office." How routine it was.

00:09:08.607 --> 00:09:14.740
The whole street covered with bodies. The reaction to that was nuts.

00:09:20.216 --> 00:09:25.020
The Iraq War logs which followed was the largest military leak in history,

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:30.280
revealing that more than 15,000 civilian deaths hadn't been publicly reported.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:36.320
And it exposed the fact that the U.S. military had ignored reports of torture, rape, and murder

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.710
by Iraqi authorities and soldiers.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:43.920
15,000 people whose deaths were recorded

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:47.930
by the U.S. military but were completely unknown to the rest of the world.

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:50.254
That's a very significant thing.

00:09:50.254 --> 00:09:53.510
And compare that to the 3000 people who died in 9/11.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:55.330
And imagine the significance.

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:59.900
Then in November, 2010, there was a leak of more than 3 million U.S. diplomatic cables,

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:02.480
revealing corruption among various Arab governments,

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:07.072
which helped inspire the Tunisian Revolution that began the Arab Spring.

00:10:07.072 --> 00:10:10.320
WikiLeaks also released thousands of pages of both CIA 

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.960
and Russian state surveillance techniques, exposed Saudi support for ISIS,

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:18.000
and undisclosed U.S. training of soldiers in Yemen,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.790
and helped provide Edward Snowden safe passage out of Hong Kong.

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:26.240
Assange says his guiding principle has been to grant regular citizens access

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.320
to the information powerful governments,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.180
corporations, and media gatekeepers wanted nobody to see.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:36.280
Someone's right to speak and someone's right to know produce a right to communicate.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:40.420
And so that is the grounding structure

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:44.730
for all that we treasure about civilized life.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:48.280
But the 2016 leaks damaging to the Hillary Clinton campaign

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:51.943
and Democratic Party found him both new allies on the Right.

00:10:51.943 --> 00:10:54.870
You have done a lot of good in what you have exposed

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:59.450
about how corrupt, dishonest, and phony our government is.

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:01.690
WikiLeaks. I love WikiLeaks.

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:05.410
And critics on the left. In terms of the Mueller Report, if we get a good look at it next week,

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:06.880
and it's not too heavily redacted,

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:10.160
we get a look at it and it shows that WikiLeaks,

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.800
Assange himself, worked with the Russians to put out their attacks

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.030
on the Democratic candidate, Hillary Clinton.

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:19.730
But Assange, who once said he viewed the choice between Clinton and Trump

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:22.490
as a choice between cholera and gonorrhea,

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:25.440
and who denies any connection to the Russian government,

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:29.900
maintains that his commitment is to bringing to light true information,

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:33.210
regardless of which political regime it might damage.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:36.620
We believe that the best type of government

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:41.050
comes from a government that is scrutinized by the people

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:44.700
when they have true information about how government and major corporations,

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.720
other power actors in society, actually behave.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:51.320
Timm says WikiLeaks' early work, which went a long way towards revealing

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.350
the nature of 21st century American warfare and surveillance

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:56.860
and exposing corrupt authoritarian governments,

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:58.930
is what even those who dislike Assange

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:01.600
should remember as he faces life in prison.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:07.060
Much like Julian Assange himself, the legacy of WikiLeaks is complicated.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:10.570
They did a lot of good for the world, especially in their early days

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:15.010
when they were releasing all sorts of really important stories and really important investigations.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:19.080
So I think people kind of forget because their mind is clouded by 2016,

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:24.870
the ability of news organizations to run secure submission systems, prioritizing digital security.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:28.070
This was kind of a thing in 2010 that nobody had even thought of.

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:29.560
And now everybody's doing it.

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.210
The ability to create these large coalitions

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:36.540
of news organizations who are going over huge data sets.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:40.268
We can look to investigations like The Panama Papers, for example,

00:12:40.268 --> 00:12:43.620
where there's a whole bunch of news organizations working together.

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:46.600
This was not something that was happening very often

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:50.550
before WikiLeaks partnered with the New York Times, The Guardian, and many other papers.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:52.540
Their legacy is certainly mixed.

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:54.790
But there's a lot of good things, a lot of lasting things,

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:59.400
that have actually come out of how WikiLeaks operated.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:03.828
Assange remains in a London prison, confined to his cell for 23 hours a day, 

00:13:03.828 --> 00:13:06.803
according to WikiLeaks Editor-in-Chief, Kristinn Hrafnsson

00:13:06.803 --> 00:13:11.610
He's awaiting a ruling from the British Extradition Court, which is scheduled for January 4th.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:17.318
Government whistleblowers such as Edward Snowden, Daniel Ellsberg, John Kiriakou, and William Binney,

00:13:17.318 --> 00:13:21.211
along with more than 7600 cosigners to an open letter,

00:13:21.211 --> 00:13:23.970
have called for President Trump to drop all charges.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:25.615
Throughout his career, however,

00:13:25.615 --> 00:13:29.704
Assange has been cynical about the notion that the democratic and judicial process

00:13:29.704 --> 00:13:34.097
can truly constrain government power and protect individual rights.

00:13:34.097 --> 00:13:36.338
The citizens of the world need to take matters

00:13:36.338 --> 00:13:41.896
into their own hands and protect themselves using encryption and other freedom-preserving tools.

00:13:41.896 --> 00:13:46.764
We will end up into a global totalitarian surveillance society.

00:13:46.764 --> 00:13:49.618
Perhaps they will just be the last free living people,

00:13:49.618 --> 00:13:52.960
those people who understand how to use this cryptography

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:56.558
to defend against this complete total surveillance.

